6️⃣ “Which table tracks the lifecycle status of an encounter?”

🔎 What this means

Inside Edifecs, every claim moves through stages like:
	•	Received
	•	Validated
	•	Error
	•	Ready for submission
	•	Batched
	•	Submitted
	•	Response received

There must be a table that stores:
	•	Encounter ID
	•	Current Status
	•	Status Code
	•	Status Date

🎯 Why we need it

Without this table:
	•	We cannot know where a claim is stuck.
	•	We cannot detect drop between Edifecs and CMS.

This is the most important table for reconciliation.

⸻

7️⃣ “Is there a status history table that shows movement across stages?”

🔎 What this means

There are two possibilities:
	•	A table that stores only the current status.
	•	OR a table that stores every status change over time.

Example:

Encounter	Status	Timestamp
12345	Received	Jan 1
12345	Validated	Jan 1
12345	Error	Jan 2
12345	Corrected	Jan 3
12345	Submitted	Jan 4

That’s status history.

🎯 Why we need it

This helps answer:
	•	How long did it stay in error?
	•	Was it corrected?
	•	Was it re-submitted?
	•	Is it delayed operationally?

For VP-level insight:

This gives process bottleneck visibility.

⸻

8️⃣ “What are the main status codes used to indicate: received, validated, error, batched, submitted?”

🔎 What this means

Edifecs likely uses coded values like:
	•	RCV
	•	VAL
	•	ERR
	•	BATCH
	•	SUB
	•	REJ
	•	ACC

We need a mapping of:
Status Code → Meaning

🎯 Why we need it

Because:
	•	Your reconciliation logic depends on interpreting these correctly.
	•	“Error” vs “Pending” vs “Ready” are very different business impacts.

Without status code definitions, your logic will be wrong.

